mmetrical positions. Specifically, if a row contains special values (like 1 or 7), those should be mirrored to the opposite side of the grid.\n\nLooking at the demo:\n- Input row 2: `[7, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4]` → Output: `[7, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 7]`\n- Input row 3: `[1, 7, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4]` → Output: `[1, 7, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 7, 1]`\n- Input row 7: `[1, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4]` → Output: `[1, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 1]`\n\nThe rule appears to be: **For each row, copy values from the rightmost position of the row to the leftmost position, and vice versa. The background value (dominant color in the grid) goes to 0.**\n\nLet me submit a corrected implementation:\n\n```python\ndef transform(grid):\n    \"\"\"Transform grid: find dominant background color, set to 0. \n    Also copy row end values to row start and vice versa.\n    \"\"\"\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Find background color (most frequent)\n    from collections import Counter\n    flat = [v for row in grid for v in row]\n    bg_color = max(set(flat), key=flat.count)\n    if len(grid) > 1 and len(grid[0]) > 1 and bg_color is None:\n        bg_color = 0\n    \n    output = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    \n    for row_idx in range(rows):\n        for col_idx in range(cols):\n            val = grid[row_idx][col_idx]\n            if val != bg_color:\n                output[row_idx][col_idx] = val\n    \n    # Add symmetry: for rows with non-background, mirror ends\n    for row_idx in range(rows):\n        first = grid[row_idx][0]\n        last = grid[row_idx][cols - 1]\n        if first != bg_color and first != last:\n            output[row_idx][cols - 1] = first\n        if last != bg_color and last != first:\n            output[row_idx][0] = last\n    \n    # Also copy from end to start position with same value\n    for row_idx in range(rows):\n        for col_idx in range(cols):\n            if col_idx == 0 and grid[ro